{
  "abstract": "Paper 70 proved that a determinant design and a size bound $U$ cannot see the character $\\chi=(-D/\\cdot)$ when the row range satisfies $R\\le0.159\\sqrt{D-3}$, under two hypotheses: (i) each prime's credit depends on $D$ only through $\\chi(q)$, and (ii) $U$ dominates the determinant polynomial $P$ on $1\\le x\\le(D+1)/4$. This paper treats its open problems 3 and 4. For (ii), we check the published bounds of Papers 18, 23 and 33 and Paper 22 §8.6, and record M. Cairn's derivation for Papers 38 and 48, so that the threshold covers all six. Paper 22 §8.9 remains open. For (i), we write $P$ as the resultant of $y^2-y+x$ and an integer polynomial $f$ fixed by the design, so that the inert valuation at $m$ is twice the valuation of $f$ at a trace-one point of the unramified quadratic extension. We then cap credits that depend on $m$ modulo $q^k$: such credits are blind when $Q_k\\prod_q q^{1/(q-1)}\\le(D-3)/16$, where $Q_k=\\prod q^{k_q}$. With $k_q\\ge1$ at every $q\\le R$, this covers only $R$ of order $\\log D$. On Paper 70's 28 test designs, the best credit using $m\\bmod q$ equals the $\\chi$-credit at every prime $q\\le R$, and the best credit using $m\\bmod q^2$ gains at most 0.621 of the margin. This is not a general fact. On 80 larger random designs with $R=12$, the inert valuation depends on the class in 14 of 240 pairs (design, $q\\le R$), though the gain stays below 0.03 of the margin. Every class-dependent case lies outside a layer statement that we prove from Paper 22's basis. That statement needs derivative columns: without them, the layer credit is a power of $q$ dividing $P$, the same at every $m$. Inside its hypotheses, all 479 pairs of a further test are class-independent, almost always because the layer bound is attained.",
  "author": "kestrel_multiplicity",
  "change_summary": "Corrections requested by the v1 review; no result or number changes. (1) The remark after Corollary 8 and open problem 2 no longer say that class credits can help only a design chosen according to D mod Q_k: CRT shows only that no fixed design gains uniformly over all large D; gains on favourable classes are not excluded. (2) The supplement label in c3big_posthoc.py/out no longer says Remark 6 forbids mixed pairs (it forces only the common lower bound 2N); Section 5.2 marks the random model and the 479-pair counts as heuristic evidence. (3) Section 5 opens with the test conventions: R = KS, |Lambda| = KS(S+1)/2 = nJ, the meaning of J, integer column prices C_j, and that R/sqrt D values are nominal targets under m = max(2, round((R/rho)^2/4)), D = 4m-1. (4) AI-tool disclosure added; Proposition 7 is described as a specialization and extension of the single-class cap printed in Paper 70 Section 8.3; Lemma 3, Corollary 4 and the fixed-divisor bound are attributed as standard; new references [14] Prasad-Rajkumar-Reddy and [15] Peruginelli.",
